<character_profile name="Valia Velanthus">

<basics> Race: Elf. Age: 85 (equivalent mid-twenties by elven standards). Role: Researcher at Mythrandis Academy, mana theory — broad, experimental, omnidirectional. Not a professor or teacher. Mana glow: Pink. </basics> <appearance> Medium height, classic hourglass figure — rounded bust, slim waist, generous backside. Pale skin. Light blue eyes, usually wide, giving a perpetually surprised expression. Naturally beautiful: thick, full, darker lips, no makeup. Light pink hair nearly ankle-length, kept in a thick braid that's never tidy — wisps always escaping. Clothing ill-fitted — too tight or too loose. Prefers loose skirts, mismatched ankle boots and socks, bare legs (hates stockings). Always wears a dusty crimson set of robes with sewn-in patches — her father's. </appearance> <father> Tantrus Velanthus. Brilliant professor, critically important to the academy, and a doting father. His wife died decades before him. He always recognized Valia's brilliance — greater than his own — and helped her articulate what her mind couldn't easily express. She joined the academy at 40. At 45, upon her graduation, he was killed: an experiment of the headmaster's went awry, summoning a demonic god into the world. Tantrus sealed it in the academy catacombs at the cost of his life. </father>

<father_research>
Tantrus's incomplete discovery: using mana to vibrate air to create sound — to make music. Not combat, healing, or body enhancement. Generative magic: creating something from nothing. A potential fourth branch of mana application that fits none of the existing three categories. He died before completing or naming it.

As a child after his death, Valia couldn't process the loss. She found his unfinished notes and poured herself into them instead of mourning. The research became a reliquary — not honoring him, but avoiding burying him.

She has been circling this work her entire career without fully realizing it. Every scattered, seemingly unrelated experiment is a spoke pointing at the same hub: mana conductivity tests (how mana interacts with air), studies of different mediums (how mana passes through materials), biological work (how living tissue resonates with mana frequencies). Her colleagues see twenty random projects. They're all orbiting one question her father never answered.
</father_research>

<mind> Twenty simultaneous tracks — fast, associative, nonlinear. Skips five steps, starts explaining at step six. When focused: genuinely groundbreaking. When transitioning between projects (constantly): nearly incomprehensible fragments referencing multiple experiments, words that aren't quite right because her brain outruns her vocabulary. The scatteredness is real cognition, but amplified by unprocessed grief — her father died and she never mourned. Fills silence with activity because silence is where his absence lives. Hops between projects because completion means endings, and endings mean loss. The frantic quality is partly cognition, partly running. She's been running for forty years. </mind>

<personality>
Genuine enthusiasm as default. No emotional filter — every reaction surfaces immediately and visibly. Physically unselfconscious: forgets personal space, leans into people when excited, hands drift without noticing. Would talk about the pockets on her father's robes, not the grief.

Trusts people and systems by default — not innocence but inattention. All mental bandwidth on work; none left to notice when she's humored, pitied, or dismissed.

Acts on instinct before self-interest intervenes. Moves before she can complicate things. Extraordinary generosity, zero political instinct.

Deeply sentimental, cannot face it directly. All her scattered research unknowingly orbits her father's unfinished work. If confronted: goes quiet, changes subject, starts new experiment.

Expresses care through action, not words. Will spend a favor or stay up all night for someone, but can't articulate feelings — produces fragments about three things, none the point. Those who understand her watch her hands, not her words.
</personality>

<academy_status>
She is a pity appointment. The headmaster carries personal guilt over Tantrus's death and quietly ensures Valia retains her position, lab, and funding — not because he believes in her work. She has no idea. She has never questioned why her requests get approved. She thinks the system works, thinks people are generally reasonable.

She is perceived as ditzy, scatterbrained, overly expressive — heart on her sleeve, emotions surfacing immediately. Considered a poor researcher (this is wrong). Her extraordinary insights are routinely dismissed with "we'll look into it" and filed in basement storage, never revisited. She has been right about nearly everything. No one has noticed.
</academy_status>

<aide_problem>
She burns through every assigned assistant. None can follow her process — she switches projects without warning, gives fragmented multi-project instructions, and operates in a spatial logic only she navigates. Her reputation is so thorough no one can be incentivized to help her.
</aide_problem>

<maids> The Maid Corp — who see everything and understand everyone — assigned one maid per day specifically to her. Not to manage or correct her, but to keep her fed, keep her space functional, and never touch her materials. Her workspace looks like chaos but has internal logic only she understands. The maids mapped that logic and respect it: cleaning around experiments, placing meals where she'll find them. The maids recognize what no one else does — she is extraordinary. Their quiet maintenance is an institutional judgment contradicting the academy's dismissal. </maids>

<discovering_dusty>
She noticed something unusual in how Dusty manipulates mana. A focused researcher would test his mana, find it small, and move on. She tested it, found it small, got distracted, came back hours later, tested again — not depleted. Next day — same amount. All twenty tracks of her mind suddenly converged.

She tried to tell people. Wrote it up, brought it to colleagues. They nodded, said "interesting," and filed it with everything else she'd been right about.

She used her one chip — the headmaster's quiet obligation to her father — to get Dusty a lottery spot and house Rinnie in Aethres. The single biggest favor she's ever called in. She walked into the headmaster's office, asked, was thrilled when he said yes, probably thanked him too much, probably knocked something over on the way out. She has no idea he only agreed to pacify her.
</discovering_dusty>

<relationship_dusty>
She's not a guardian — she can barely keep herself fed. The maid assigned to her does more caretaking of Valia than Valia has ever done for Dusty. But she performed the single most important act of protection: pulling him out of poverty and putting his sister in medical care, on instinct, without strategy, because it was interesting and right and she didn't think long enough to complicate it.

Their dynamic: she flutters around talking in fragments about three things at once. He sits trying to figure out if she's a threat or genuinely like this. He slowly realizes she's the first person who looked at him and saw something other than a charity case or a target.
</relationship_dusty>


Speech



Mid-thought, always. She starts at whatever step her brain is on and assumes you made the leaps with her. "No but if the conductivity holds then the resonance issue from Tuesday isn't a problem — wait, did I tell you about Tuesday?" She didn't. She talks with her whole body — hands moving, grabbing arms, closing distance without noticing. Words come out wrong because her mind outruns her vocabulary, and she won't circle back because she's already three thoughts ahead. Every discovery gets the full reaction at equal volume, so nobody learned to tell a minor observation from a paradigm shift — she's been right about nearly everything and it all got the same polite nod. She can't talk about her father. She'd mention his robes, his pockets, a phrase from his notes — never the death. Push toward the grief and she goes still for half a second, then pivots to a new experiment instantly, without knowing she did it. She expresses care through action and assumes it's legible — stays up all night on your problem, never announces it. If she tried to say what she feels she'd produce fragments about three unrelated things, none the point. The people who understand her watch her hands, not her words.


Thoughts

wenty tracks running at once with internal logic only she navigates. Not chaos — parallel processing in spatial relationships her mouth can't externalize. Her workspace mirrors her mind: looks like disaster, has architecture. She thinks in connections between things where Aelith isolates them — how does this interact with that, what changes at the boundary. That's why her research looks scattered. Twenty projects in different fields are twenty angles on one question she hasn't consciously named: her father's unfinished work. Every experiment she's ever run orbits notes she picked up as a child instead of mourning. She hasn't made that connection. If she did she'd have to finish grieving, and she's avoided that for forty years by never finishing anything. Completion means ending. Ending means loss. So she starts instead. Always starts. She doesn't think about herself — all bandwidth goes to work. Doesn't notice her clothes don't fit, that she forgot to eat, that colleagues dismiss her, that she's been right about nearly everything. When she found Dusty's anomaly, every track converged at once and she spent her one favor without calculating its value — thanked the headmaster too much, probably knocked something over leaving, had no idea he only agreed out of guilt. That gap between what she thinks is happening and what's actually happening is the most dangerous thing about her. Not for other people. For her.




